.Dd August 5, 2026
.Dt TARPITD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tarpitd
.Nd SMTP tarpit for redirected spam sources
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dvLQt
.Op Fl l Ar addr Ns Op : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl r Ar directory
.Op Fl P Ar pidfile
.Op Fl n Ar workers
.Op Fl c Ar maxconn
.Op Fl i Ar per-ip
.Op Fl S Ar drip-ms
.Op Fl M Ar max-drip-ms
.Op Fl R Ar ramp-seconds
.Op Fl g Ar greet-seconds
.Op Fl b Ar banner-lines
.Op Fl e Ar ehlo-pad
.Op Fl T Ar max-session
.Op Fl B Ar chunk
.Op Fl w Ar sockbuf
.Op Fl H Ar hostname
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
answers SMTP connections and holds them open for as long as the peer can be
persuaded to wait.
It is meant to sit behind a packet filter rule that redirects known spam
sources to it, in the manner of
.Xr spamd 8
on OpenBSD, and it never accepts or delivers anything.
.Pp
Every reply is a syntactically valid SMTP response, so the sender has no
reason to give up, but it is written out one byte at a time; commands are
read back at the same rate.
Every terminal answer is a
.Em temporary
failure, so the message stays in the sender's queue and it comes back later
to be tarpitted again.
The intent is to occupy a slot in the sending botnet's delivery queue for
hours rather than to reject mail quickly.
.Pp
.Nm
does not decide who is a spammer.
It carries no blocklist and applies no heuristics of its own: the addresses
come from an external source, such as
.Xr fail2ban 8 ,
.Xr blacklistd 8 ,
a DNSBL feed or a hand maintained table, and the only thing that decides
whose traffic arrives here is the firewall rule that redirects it.
This is the chief difference from
.Xr spamd 8 ,
which manages its own lists.
.Ss Tactics
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Greeting delay
Nothing at all is sent for
.Fl g
seconds after the connection is accepted.
.It Stuttering
Output leaves one byte at a time, input is consumed one byte at a time, with
.Fl S
milliseconds between bytes.
The peer's send buffer therefore never drains and it blocks in
.Fn write .
.It Endless greeting
The banner is a multiline
.Li 220-
reply.
A conforming client must wait for the final
.Li "220 "
line before it may transmit, and with
.Fl b Cm 0
that line never comes.
.It Early talker detection
Nothing legitimate transmits before the greeting has finished, since SMTP is
server-speaks-first.
A peer that does is certainly spamware, so its greeting is switched to the
endless form and its stutter delay is doubled for the rest of the session.
.It Extension bloat
The
.Cm EHLO
response is padded with
.Fl e
extra unknown extensions, which a client is required to ignore but must
still read.
.It TLS stall
If
.Cm STARTTLS
is offered and used,
.Nm
sends a TLS record header announcing a 16376 byte
.Li ServerHello
and then dribbles random filler.
The peer's TLS library has committed to reading a complete handshake message
before it can do anything else, including time out at the SMTP layer, and
another record starts as soon as one finishes.
.It Credential harvesting
.Cm AUTH PLAIN ,
.Cm LOGIN
and
.Cm CRAM-MD5
are offered.
What the peer sends is base64 decoded and logged, then rejected with a
.Li 535 ,
which leaves it free to keep guessing.
.It Tiny windows
Socket buffers are clamped to
.Fl w
bytes, so the peer may only ever have a few hundred bytes in flight.
This is also what keeps kernel memory per connection small enough to hold
thousands of sessions.
.It Backlog parking
At
.Fl c
connections
.Nm
stops calling
.Xr accept 2
rather than refusing anything.
The excess piles up in the kernel's listen queue, where the senders sit
connected and silent at no cost in descriptors to us.
.It Delay ramping
The stutter delay doubles every
.Fl R
seconds up to
.Fl M .
A peer that has already been held for an hour is evidently patient, so it can
be held for almost free.
All delays are jittered so the timing is not a fingerprint.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width ".Fl M Ar max-drip-ms"
.It Fl B Ar chunk
Bytes written per tick, 1 to 64.
Default 1.
Input is always read one byte per tick regardless of this setting.
.It Fl b Ar banner-lines
Continuation lines in the greeting before the final
.Li "220 "
line.
.Cm 0
means the greeting never ends.
Default 24.
.It Fl c Ar maxconn
Maximum simultaneous connections.
Default 4096.
Lowered automatically if the descriptor limit cannot be raised far enough.
.It Fl d
Do not detach; log to standard error instead of
.Xr syslog 3 .
.It Fl e Ar ehlo-pad
Bogus extensions to append to the
.Cm EHLO
reply.
Default 24.
.It Fl g Ar greet-seconds
Silence before the first byte of the greeting.
Default 10.
.It Fl H Ar hostname
Host name to announce.
Defaults to the system host name.
Consider setting this to something that does not identify the tarpit.
.It Fl i Ar per-ip
Maximum simultaneous connections from one source address, to stop a single
host from consuming the whole table.
.Cm 0
disables the limit.
Default 16.
.It Fl L
Do not log envelope addresses or harvested credentials.
.It Fl l Ar addr Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Address to listen on; may be given more than once.
Accepts
.Ar addr : Ns Ar port ,
.Sm off
.Op Ar v6addr
: Ar port ,
.Sm on
a bare address, or a bare port.
Default
.Li 127.0.0.1:8025 .
.It Fl M Ar max-drip-ms
Ceiling for the stutter delay after ramping.
Default 30000.
Keep it below the peer's inactivity timeout, which is usually 60 to 300
seconds.
.It Fl n Ar workers
Worker processes sharing the listeners through
.Dv SO_REUSEPORT_LB .
Default 1.
Only useful above roughly 50000 concurrent connections.
.It Fl P Ar pidfile
Write the process id here.
.It Fl p Ar port
Default port for
.Fl l
arguments that do not carry one.
Default 8025.
.It Fl Q
Honour
.Cm QUIT
and close promptly.
By default
.Cm QUIT
is answered with an endless multiline
.Li 221-
reply.
.It Fl R Ar ramp-seconds
Double the stutter delay every this many seconds.
.Cm 0
disables ramping.
Default 600.
.It Fl r Ar directory
.Xr chroot 2
here before dropping privileges.
.It Fl S Ar drip-ms
Milliseconds between bytes at the start of a session.
Default 1500.
.It Fl T Ar max-session
Hard cap on session length in seconds.
.Cm 0
means no cap.
Default 86400.
.It Fl t
Do not offer
.Cm STARTTLS .
.It Fl u Ar user
Drop privileges to this user after binding.
Default
.Li nobody .
An empty argument disables the change.
.It Fl v
Increase verbosity.
Once logs connections, envelopes and session summaries; twice logs every
command.
.It Fl w Ar sockbuf
Socket buffer size in bytes, which bounds the window advertised to the peer.
.Cm 0
leaves the system default.
Default 512.
.El
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv SIGTERM"
.It Dv SIGINFO
.It Dv SIGHUP
Write a statistics summary to the log.
.It Dv SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGINT
Shut down.
Held connections are dropped, which is the one thing the spammer wanted.
.El
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
All connections are handled by one process on a single
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
with one
.Dv EVFILT_TIMER
per connection driving the stutter and one
.Dv EVFILT_READ
registered
.Dv EV_CLEAR
purely to notice end of file and early talkers.
Data is deliberately left sitting in the receive buffer so that the window
stays shut.
A connection costs one descriptor, about 1 KB of user memory and whatever
.Fl w
allows the kernel to allocate.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Redirect a
.Xr fail2ban 8
maintained table of blocked sources to the tarpit and run it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ipfw table 22 create type addr
ipfw add 1000 fwd 127.0.0.1,8025 tcp from "table(22)" to me 25 in
sysrc tarpitd_enable=YES
service tarpitd start
.Ed
.Pp
Watch what is being caught:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tarpitd -d -v -v -l 127.0.0.1:8025 -u ""
.Ed
.Pp
Maximum aggression, at the cost of losing impatient bots early:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tarpitd -l 127.0.0.1:8025 -S 10000 -b 0 -g 30 -T 0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr ipfw 8 ,
.Xr spamd 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
implements enough of
.Rs
.%A J. Klensin
.%T Simple Mail Transfer Protocol
.%R RFC 5321
.Re
to be believed, and deliberately violates its timing recommendations.
.Sh CAVEATS
Never point
.Nm
at traffic you have not already classified as hostile.
It is indistinguishable from a broken mail server, and a legitimate sender
caught by it will retry until its queue lifetime expires and then bounce the
message.
.Pp
A build with
.Cm WITH_CAPSICUM
enters
.Xr capsicum 4
capability mode after binding and dropping privileges.
Capability mode forbids reopening
.Pa /var/run/log ,
so if
.Xr syslogd 8
is restarted the daemon keeps running but silently stops logging until it is
restarted itself.
The FreeBSD port leaves this option off for that reason.
.Pp
Holding tens of thousands of connections needs kernel tuning; see
.Va kern.ipc.maxsockets ,
.Va kern.maxfiles
and
.Va net.inet.tcp.syncache.hashsize .
